WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.267
Mentoring.

2
00:00:01.547 --> 00:00:05.643
Day 76 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.393 --> 00:00:08.417
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.697 --> 00:00:12.751
Mentoring is one of the fastest ways to grow, whichever side you're on.

5
00:00:13.031 --> 00:00:21.052
A good mentee asks better questions, and a good mentor learns to explain clearly, listen properly, and let other people succeed.

6
00:00:21.332 --> 00:00:23.081
This page covers both sides.

7
00:00:24.431 --> 00:00:25.199
Info:

8
00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:26.674
Related pages.

9
00:00:26.954 --> 00:00:31.071
Talking to Juniors covers day-to-day teaching and code-review feedback.

10
00:00:31.351 --> 00:00:37.453
This page is about structured, longer-term mentoring relationships, inside or outside your team.

11
00:00:38.803 --> 00:00:39.656
Part 1.

12
00:00:39.936 --> 00:00:42.411
Mentor, coach, sponsor: what's the difference?

13
00:00:43.661 --> 00:00:45.026
Here is a quick table:

14
00:00:45.306 --> 00:00:47.546
Role, and What they do, and Example.

15
00:00:47.826 --> 00:00:48.658
Mentor.

16
00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:49.834
What they do:

17
00:00:50.114 --> 00:00:51.885
Shares experience and advice.

18
00:00:52.165 --> 00:00:53.039
Example:

19
00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.242
When I moved to tech lead, here's what I wish I'd known...

20
00:00:56.522 --> 00:00:57.247
Coach.

21
00:00:57.527 --> 00:00:58.423
What they do:

22
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:00.986
Asks questions so you find the answer.

23
00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:02.141
Example:

24
00:01:02.421 --> 00:01:03.786
What options do you see?

25
00:01:04.066 --> 00:01:05.175
What's stopping you?

26
00:01:05.455 --> 00:01:06.394
Sponsor.

27
00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:07.570
What they do:

28
00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:10.751
Uses their influence for you when you're not in the room.

29
00:01:11.031 --> 00:01:11.906
Example:

30
00:01:12.186 --> 00:01:13.999
Kavya should lead the new project.

31
00:01:14.279 --> 00:01:15.197
She's ready.

32
00:01:15.477 --> 00:01:16.159
Buddy.

33
00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:17.335
What they do:

34
00:01:17.615 --> 00:01:19.237
Helps a new joiner settle in.

35
00:01:19.517 --> 00:01:20.391
Example:

36
00:01:20.671 --> 00:01:22.997
Here's how deploys work; let's grab lunch.

37
00:01:24.347 --> 00:01:27.120
Good mentors move between all of these as needed.

38
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.387
Step 1:

39
00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:32.462
Agree: goals, frequency, how you'll communicate.

40
00:01:32.742 --> 00:01:33.659
Step 2:

41
00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:35.049
Meet regularly:

42
00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:38.294
30 to 45 minutes, every 2 to 4 weeks.

43
00:01:38.574 --> 00:01:39.491
Step 3:

44
00:01:39.771 --> 00:01:42.246
Act: the mentee tries things between sessions.

45
00:01:42.526 --> 00:01:43.486
Step 4:

46
00:01:43.766 --> 00:01:46.134
Reflect: what worked, what didn't, what's next.

47
00:01:46.414 --> 00:01:47.374
Step 5:

48
00:01:47.654 --> 00:01:52.817
Review and close: after 3 to 6 months, celebrate and decide whether to continue.

49
00:01:54.167 --> 00:01:55.233
Being a mentee.

50
00:01:55.513 --> 00:01:56.388
Part 2.

51
00:01:56.668 --> 00:01:57.884
Finding a mentor.

52
00:01:59.134 --> 00:02:05.705
Look for someone 1 to 2 steps ahead of you on the path you want, not necessarily the most senior person you know.

53
00:02:05.985 --> 00:02:10.145
Look at your company's other teams, ex-colleagues, meetups and communities.

54
00:02:11.495 --> 00:02:12.433
You could say:

55
00:02:12.713 --> 00:02:16.063
Hi Meena, I really liked your talk on event-driven design.

56
00:02:16.343 --> 00:02:20.439
I'm working towards a senior backend role, and I'd value your perspective.

57
00:02:20.719 --> 00:02:24.772
Would you be open to a 30-minute chat once a month for the next 3 months?

58
00:02:25.052 --> 00:02:29.596
I'll come prepared with specific questions, and I'm happy to work around your schedule.

59
00:02:30.946 --> 00:02:39.287
Why this works: it's specific about why you chose them, it has a clear goal, the time ask is limited, and you've shown you'll make it easy for them.

60
00:02:40.637 --> 00:02:41.277
Tip:

61
00:02:41.557 --> 00:02:42.539
Start small.

62
00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:44.739
Don't open with Will you be my mentor?

63
00:02:45.019 --> 00:02:46.768
That feels like a big commitment.

64
00:02:47.048 --> 00:02:50.120
Ask for one conversation about a specific topic.

65
00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.384
If it goes well, ask for another.

66
00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:54.630
Part 3.

67
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.425
Preparing for each session.

68
00:02:57.675 --> 00:02:59.552
Here is a template you can copy.

69
00:02:59.832 --> 00:03:01.325
Mentoring session, Date.

70
00:03:01.605 --> 00:03:07.195
Since last time: led my first design review (went OK; lost control of the discussion).

71
00:03:07.475 --> 00:03:12.083
Question 1: how do you keep a design review on track when two seniors argue?

72
00:03:12.363 --> 00:03:16.992
Question 2: should I learn Kubernetes deeply or stay focused on backend?

73
00:03:17.272 --> 00:03:20.920
Want feedback on: my design doc for the slot service (link).

74
00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:24.687
Part 4.

75
00:03:24.967 --> 00:03:26.823
Questions worth asking a mentor.

76
00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:29.972
What do you wish you'd known at my stage?

77
00:03:30.252 --> 00:03:33.900
What separates good engineers from great ones, in your experience?

78
00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:36.335
How did you handle specific situation?

79
00:03:36.615 --> 00:03:38.151
What would you do in my position?

80
00:03:38.431 --> 00:03:40.351
What am I not asking that I should be?

81
00:03:40.631 --> 00:03:43.703
Can you review this doc or code or talk and be honest?

82
00:03:45.053 --> 00:03:46.013
Part 5.

83
00:03:46.293 --> 00:03:47.573
Being a good mentee.

84
00:03:48.823 --> 00:03:49.527
Do:

85
00:03:49.807 --> 00:03:54.095
Own the relationship: schedule the sessions, send the agenda, follow up.

86
00:03:54.375 --> 00:03:55.079
Do:

87
00:03:55.359 --> 00:03:57.279
Try their advice and report back:

88
00:03:57.559 --> 00:03:59.884
I tried your suggestion; here's what happened.

89
00:04:00.164 --> 00:04:00.868
Do:

90
00:04:01.148 --> 00:04:04.881
Respect their time: finish on time; cancel early if you must.

91
00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:05.865
Do:

92
00:04:06.145 --> 00:04:07.660
Say thank you specifically:

93
00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.311
Your advice on design reviews really helped me last week.

94
00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:12.444
Avoid:

95
00:04:12.724 --> 00:04:15.988
Expecting them to solve your problems or get you promoted.

96
00:04:16.268 --> 00:04:17.121
Avoid:

97
00:04:17.401 --> 00:04:19.513
Only getting in touch when you need something.

98
00:04:20.863 --> 00:04:21.802
Part 6.

99
00:04:22.082 --> 00:04:23.234
Ending gracefully.

100
00:04:24.484 --> 00:04:25.423
You could say:

101
00:04:25.703 --> 00:04:28.220
These last six months have been really valuable.

102
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:31.657
I've led three design reviews and I'm much more confident.

103
00:04:31.937 --> 00:04:32.769
Thank you.

104
00:04:33.049 --> 00:04:37.337
I'd love to stay in touch, and I'll let you know how the promotion cycle goes.

105
00:04:38.687 --> 00:04:39.754
Being a mentor.

106
00:04:40.034 --> 00:04:40.973
Part 7.

107
00:04:41.253 --> 00:04:42.341
The first session.

108
00:04:43.591 --> 00:04:44.529
You could say:

109
00:04:44.809 --> 00:04:46.729
Let's start with what you want from this.

110
00:04:47.009 --> 00:04:50.764
Where would you like to be in 6 months, and what's getting in the way right now?

111
00:04:51.044 --> 00:04:54.457
Also, how do you like to get feedback: direct, or gentler?

112
00:04:55.807 --> 00:04:56.703
Agree on:

113
00:04:56.983 --> 00:04:57.815
Goals:

114
00:04:58.095 --> 00:05:04.261
1 to 3 specific outcomes (for example, lead a design review, get promoted to SDE-2).

115
00:05:04.541 --> 00:05:07.613
Rhythm: how often, how long, video or in person.

116
00:05:07.893 --> 00:05:10.623
Confidentiality: what's said stays between you.

117
00:05:10.903 --> 00:05:11.757
Honesty:

118
00:05:12.037 --> 00:05:15.002
I'll be direct with you; tell me if something isn't helping.

119
00:05:16.352 --> 00:05:17.227
Part 8.

120
00:05:17.507 --> 00:05:20.003
Listen more, advise less (the GROW model).

121
00:05:21.253 --> 00:05:22.618
Here is a quick table:

122
00:05:22.898 --> 00:05:24.071
Step, and Question.

123
00:05:24.351 --> 00:05:25.055
Goal:

124
00:05:25.335 --> 00:05:27.618
What do you want to get out of today's conversation?

125
00:05:27.898 --> 00:05:28.773
Reality:

126
00:05:29.053 --> 00:05:30.439
What's happening right now?

127
00:05:30.719 --> 00:05:31.850
What have you tried?

128
00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.005
Options:

129
00:05:33.285 --> 00:05:34.245
What could you do?

130
00:05:34.525 --> 00:05:35.357
What else?

131
00:05:35.637 --> 00:05:36.639
Way forward:

132
00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:38.583
What will you do, and by when?

133
00:05:39.933 --> 00:05:41.192
Ask before telling.

134
00:05:41.472 --> 00:05:44.245
Give your own advice only after they've thought it through.

135
00:05:44.525 --> 00:05:45.976
Then share it as a story:

136
00:05:46.256 --> 00:05:50.011
When I faced this, here's what I did, and here's what I'd do differently.

137
00:05:51.361 --> 00:05:52.257
Part 9.

138
00:05:52.537 --> 00:05:54.115
Useful mentor phrases.

139
00:05:55.365 --> 00:05:56.731
Here is a quick table:

140
00:05:57.011 --> 00:05:58.397
Situation, and Say.

141
00:05:58.677 --> 00:05:59.872
They want the answer:

142
00:06:00.152 --> 00:06:02.733
I'll share my view, but first, what's your instinct?

143
00:06:03.013 --> 00:06:04.528
They're too hard on themselves:

144
00:06:04.808 --> 00:06:06.899
What would you tell a friend in this situation?

145
00:06:07.179 --> 00:06:08.480
They're avoiding something:

146
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.701
I notice we keep coming back to this.

147
00:06:10.981 --> 00:06:12.176
What makes it hard?

148
00:06:12.456 --> 00:06:13.779
Giving hard feedback:

149
00:06:14.059 --> 00:06:16.939
Can I share something that might be uncomfortable to hear?

150
00:06:17.219 --> 00:06:18.221
They succeeded:

151
00:06:18.501 --> 00:06:19.867
That's a real step up.

152
00:06:20.147 --> 00:06:21.896
What did you do differently this time?

153
00:06:22.176 --> 00:06:23.072
You don't know:

154
00:06:23.352 --> 00:06:24.803
I haven't faced that one.

155
00:06:25.083 --> 00:06:27.045
Let me introduce you to someone who has.

156
00:06:28.395 --> 00:06:29.249
Part 10.

157
00:06:29.529 --> 00:06:30.766
Go beyond advice.

158
00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:35.067
Open doors: introduce them to people they should know.

159
00:06:35.347 --> 00:06:36.520
Give visibility:

160
00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:39.680
Would you like to present this at the architecture review?

161
00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:42.691
Sponsor: speak their name in rooms they aren't in.

162
00:06:42.971 --> 00:06:46.469
Share your mistakes: your failures teach more than your successes.

163
00:06:47.819 --> 00:06:48.822
Part 11.

164
00:06:49.102 --> 00:06:50.339
Mentor mistakes.

165
00:06:51.589 --> 00:06:52.443
Avoid:

166
00:06:52.723 --> 00:06:55.347
Turning every session into stories about yourself.

167
00:06:55.627 --> 00:06:56.480
Avoid:

168
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.832
Creating a mini-me: their path doesn't have to be yours.

169
00:07:00.112 --> 00:07:00.965
Avoid:

170
00:07:01.245 --> 00:07:03.933
Solving their problems for them (they learn nothing).

171
00:07:04.213 --> 00:07:05.067
Avoid:

172
00:07:05.347 --> 00:07:07.608
Breaking confidence, even casually.

173
00:07:07.888 --> 00:07:08.741
Avoid:

174
00:07:09.021 --> 00:07:12.520
Cancelling repeatedly; it signals they aren't a priority.

175
00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:14.510
Tip:

176
00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:16.561
You're never too junior to mentor.

177
00:07:16.841 --> 00:07:26.718
A 2-year engineer can mentor an intern, and a junior can reverse-mentor a senior on new tools, AI workflows, or what new joiners find confusing.

178
00:07:28.068 --> 00:07:29.007
Part 12.

179
00:07:29.287 --> 00:07:30.759
Group and peer mentoring.

180
00:07:32.009 --> 00:07:33.054
Peer circles:

181
00:07:33.334 --> 00:07:37.899
4 to 6 people at the same level meet monthly and each brings one challenge.

182
00:07:38.179 --> 00:07:44.451
Mentoring office hours: a senior engineer holds an open hour every week; anyone can drop in.

183
00:07:44.731 --> 00:07:50.257
Book or paper club: read one chapter or article, discuss how it applies to your work.

184
00:07:50.537 --> 00:07:53.801
Communities: meetups, open source, online forums.

185
00:07:54.081 --> 00:07:56.385
Mentors don't have to be at your company.

186
00:07:57.735 --> 00:07:58.780
Part 13.

187
00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.447
Practice conversation:

188
00:08:00.727 --> 00:08:02.689
First conversation with a mentor.

189
00:08:03.939 --> 00:08:05.966
Here is how the conversation goes.

190
00:08:06.246 --> 00:08:08.422
Thank you for agreeing to mentor me.

191
00:08:08.702 --> 00:08:10.131
I know you're busy.

192
00:08:10.411 --> 00:08:11.777
Happy to.

193
00:08:12.057 --> 00:08:13.763
What would you like to get out of this?

194
00:08:14.043 --> 00:08:17.051
I want to grow into a senior role in the next year.

195
00:08:17.331 --> 00:08:20.979
I think my weak area is leading design discussions.

196
00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:23.521
What happens in design discussions now?

197
00:08:24.871 --> 00:08:26.471
The conversation continues.

198
00:08:26.751 --> 00:08:33.023
I usually have ideas, but I don't speak until the end, and then the decision is already made.

199
00:08:33.303 --> 00:08:34.391
That's common.

200
00:08:34.671 --> 00:08:38.468
What if, in your next design review, you share your view in the first ten minutes?

201
00:08:38.748 --> 00:08:40.561
That's scary, but I'll try.

202
00:08:40.841 --> 00:08:44.127
Can we meet again in two weeks to talk about how it went?

203
00:08:44.407 --> 00:08:45.111
Sure.

204
00:08:45.391 --> 00:08:47.076
Send me the date of the review.

205
00:08:48.426 --> 00:08:49.962
That's the end of this lesson.

206
00:08:50.242 --> 00:08:54.914
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

207
00:08:55.194 --> 00:09:01.295
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

